{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS004913","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates are direct matches to the target trait (trait_efo = alcoholic liver cirrhosis) with broadly aligned endpoints (Alcoholic/Alcohol-associated cirrhosis). However, none reports a clearly PRS-only AUROC/AUC, so the available discrimination metrics are not directly comparable across models and limit certainty.

PGS004913 is preferred because it is explicitly designed for alcohol-associated cirrhosis among heavy drinkers with European ancestry, with phenotype wording closely aligned to the target and PRS-focused reporting (PRSice-2; 20 variants) including an R²=0.029 and an OR (top vs bottom PRS deciles)=4.4 (1.56–12.44). In contrast, PGS000704 reports only a full-model C-index=0.711 from a time-to-event model with covariates (age as time scale, sex, batch, PCs), which domain guidance cautions should not be substituted for PRS-only performance; its per-SD HR=1.18 suggests only modest PRS signal. PGS001777 shows a strong extreme-quantile OR (top 20% vs bottom 20%)=6.18 but is based on only 3 variants, has unknown covariates, a small validation sample (n=1,390), and phenotyping reported as cirrhosis in males, making its evidence package less robust.

Main limitation: because the reported AUC/C-index values are full-model and covariate sets differ/are unknown, and PRS-only discrimination is not provided, the ranking remains uncertain despite direct phenotype alignment."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002034",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (PGS001826, PGS002034), both mapped to the target EFO term and sharing the same reported/phenotyped endpoint (“Congenital deficiency of other clotting factors (including factor VII)”), same publication family, same training cohort (UKB; n=391,124), similar ancestry evaluation (EUR/SAS), and same covariate set (sex, age, birth date, deprivation index, 16 PCs). Their only visible performance signal is essentially identical partial correlation (0.0562 vs 0.0563) with overlapping CIs, and no PRS-comparable AUC/R2 is provided, limiting discrimination between them. Given the near-tie on endpoint and performance, PGS002034 is mildly preferred on structural grounds (LDpred2 genome-wide shrinkage with far more variants: 32,552 vs 45), but the lack of richer PRS-only metrics keeps confidence moderate and the overall direct-match recommendation sub-optimal."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001814","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait (nodular goiter), including two models explicitly for nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) and several for benign nodular goiter (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional candidates (PGS005273–PGS005274) are case-control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter and are less endpoint-faithful for nodular goiter itself.

PGS001814 is preferred because it has the closest phenotype alignment to the target via multinodular goiter and provides PRS-comparable performance evidence (partial correlation 0.0277 in European validation) with clearly stated basic covariates (sex, age, birth date, deprivation index, PCs). Between the two multinodular-goiter siblings, PGS001814 shows slightly higher partial-r than PGS002022 (0.0277 vs 0.024) while sharing the same study family, endpoint label, and evaluation context.

However, confidence is not high because the multinodular-goiter models come from a UK Biobank portability framework paper (a known weaker study archetype for external generalization per the provided domain guidance), and the benign nodular goiter models—while disease-focused and multi-cohort—report only full-model AUROC and have unknown covariates, making their discrimination evidence not directly comparable to PRS-only metrics. This leaves unresolved uncertainty about whether the multi-cohort benign nodular goiter scores would outperform the portability multinodular-goiter score in a PRS-only setting."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001536",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct label matches to vitiligo (trait_reported and trait_efo). However, endpoint fidelity differs: PGS000738 has phenotyping_reported=\"Red hair\" (explicit mismatch to vitiligo), and PGS000760 is evaluated in a therapy-specific context (phenotyping_reported=\"anti-PD-L1 induced hypothyroidism in cancer patients\"), making its vitiligo label less clearly aligned to generic vitiligo risk. PGS001536 has the most coherent endpoint field (phenotyping_reported=\"TTE vitiligo\") and also provides the cleanest PRS-comparable performance evidence (PGS-only AUROC=0.64193; PGS-only R2=0.01621), with basic covariates and a heritability-sane R2 (well below the provided h2_obs ceiling). Limitation: PGS001536 is a time-to-event phenotype from a pan-trait UK Biobank framework paper (snpnet, sparse model), which domain guidance flags as potentially less externally robust than disease-focused vitiligo studies; given the other two candidates’ endpoint incoherence, it is still the best-supported direct match but not high-confidence."
}
